WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.292
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:08.897
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:15.843
Unit nine: Social Movements. Today you will describe collective action with gerund phrases as subjects.

4
00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:18.761
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.759
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:24.850
Social movement.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.439
Social movement means an organised effort by many people to bring about social change.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.118
The civil rights movement transformed American law.

9
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:37.265
Your turn. Say: Social movement.

10
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.415
Social movement.

11
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.256
Grassroots.

12
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.084
Grassroots means organised by ordinary people at a local level.

13
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:51.426
The campaign began as a grassroots initiative.

14
00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:55.087
Your turn. Say: Grassroots.

15
00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:58.969
Grassroots.

16
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:00.834
Advocacy.

17
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.138
Advocacy means public support for a cause or policy.

18
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.667
Advocacy groups lobbied for safer factories.

19
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:11.982
Your turn. Say: Advocacy.

20
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:15.904
Advocacy.

21
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.872
Solidarity.

22
00:01:18.883 --> 00:01:22.276
Solidarity means unity and mutual support within a group.

23
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.579
Workers showed solidarity during the strike.

24
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:29.091
Your turn. Say: Solidarity.

25
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:33.225
Solidarity.

26
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:35.170
Collective action.

27
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:40.296
Collective action means action taken together by a group to achieve a shared goal.

28
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:43.638
Collective action forced the company to negotiate.

29
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.285
Your turn. Say: Collective action.

30
00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:51.343
Collective action.

31
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:52.957
Reform.

32
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.925
Reform means change intended to improve a system.

33
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.853
The movement won important labour reforms.

34
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.870
Your turn. Say: Reform.

35
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:06.653
Reform.

36
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:09.068
Awareness campaign.

37
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:13.662
Awareness campaign means an organised effort to inform the public.

38
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.923
An awareness campaign reduced plastic use.

39
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:20.517
Your turn. Say: Awareness campaign.

40
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:25.018
Awareness campaign.

41
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:26.784
Momentum.

42
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:31.257
Momentum means the force that keeps a process moving forward.

43
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.082
The protest gained momentum online.

44
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:37.162
Your turn. Say: Momentum.

45
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.024
Momentum.

46
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:45.414
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:52.002
Visal, you studied the campaign for better conditions in Cambodian garment factories. How did it succeed?

48
00:02:53.117 --> 00:03:02.294
Building solidarity took sustained collective action. Organising workers across different factories was the hardest part.

49
00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.302
Did international pressure help?

50
00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:16.824
Very much. Publicising conditions abroad put pressure on brands, and linking local unions with global advocacy groups gave the movement momentum.

51
00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:19.870
Why do some movements fade away?

52
00:03:20.983 --> 00:03:30.033
Relying only on online posts rarely works. Maintaining a movement requires leadership, clear demands and patience.

53
00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:32.946
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:37.292
Grammar focus: Gerund phrases as subjects.

55
00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:38.849
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.780
Building solidarity takes sustained collective action.

57
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:47.564
Linking local unions with global groups gave the movement momentum.

58
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.471
Relying only on online posts rarely works.

59
00:03:52.433 --> 00:04:02.504
A gerund phrase used as a subject is singular, however long it is: 'Organising workers across many factories IS difficult.' This structure sounds more formal than 'It is difficult to…'.

60
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:06.959
Be careful! Don't say: Build solidarity take time.

61
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.991
Say: Building solidarity takes time.

62
00:04:12.291 --> 00:04:14.906
Gerund subject + singular verb.

63
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.992
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.022
How Movements Win.

65
00:04:20.322 --> 00:04:26.153
Social movements emerge when people believe that existing institutions will not address their grievances.

66
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:31.349
Mobilizing supporters, however, requires far more than shared anger.

67
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:37.932
Framing the problem clearly and proposing concrete reforms gives a movement direction and credibility.

68
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:44.265
Sustaining momentum over years is often harder than starting a protest in a single day.

69
00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:51.306
Studying successful movements shows that solidarity across different groups is usually the decisive factor.

70
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.379
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.217 --> 00:04:59.506
Organising strikes blank courage.

72
00:04:59.806 --> 00:05:03.773
A, require. B, requires. C, requiring.

73
00:05:07.333 --> 00:05:09.252
The answer is B: requires.

74
00:05:10.417 --> 00:05:12.370
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:18.517
A, Build trust takes time.. B, Building trust takes time.. C, Building trust take time..

76
00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:24.682
The answer is B: Building trust takes time..

77
00:05:25.817 --> 00:05:29.186
A campaign organised by ordinary local people is blank.

78
00:05:29.486 --> 00:05:33.511
A, grassroots. B, advocacy. C, momentum.

79
00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:38.950
The answer is A: grassroots.

80
00:05:40.117 --> 00:05:42.093
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:42.393 --> 00:05:46.592
A, gain momentum. B, win momentum. C, make momentum.

82
00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.179
The answer is A: gain momentum.

83
00:05:53.317 --> 00:05:56.524
According to the reading, what is usually the decisive factor?

84
00:05:56.824 --> 00:06:03.078
A, online posts. B, solidarity across groups. C, a single protest.

85
00:06:06.633 --> 00:06:09.678
The answer is B: solidarity across groups.

86
00:06:10.867 --> 00:06:13.798
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.252
What social movement do you find most inspiring, and why?

88
00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:36.585
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.885 --> 00:07:14.236
The social movement I find most inspiring is the global campaign to ban landmines, which had a special impact on Cambodia. Bringing survivors, doctors and activists together created powerful solidarity across many countries. Their advocacy led to the Ottawa Treaty in 1997, and clearing mines has since saved countless lives here. Some critics point out that major powers still have not signed it. Even so, I think it proves that ordinary people working together can change international law.

90
00:07:14.536 --> 00:07:16.303
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.217 --> 00:07:21.924
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and gerund phrases as subjects.

92
00:07:22.224 --> 00:07:26.966
Today's value is solidarity. Stand together with others who face injustice.

93
00:07:27.266 --> 00:07:29.826
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.126 --> 00:07:33.080
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

